   "id": "math-0016",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "To find the probability of getting exactly 2 heads in 5 flips of a coin with probability of heads $p = \\frac{2}{3}$, I use the **binomial distribution**:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\nwhere $n = 5$, $k = 2$, and $p = \\frac{2}{3}$, so $1-p = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\n**Step 1:** Calculate the binomial coefficient:\n$$\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10$$\n\n**Step 2:** Calculate the probability terms:\n$$\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{9}, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{27}$$\n\n**Step 3:** Multiply them together:\n$$P(X = 2) = 10 \\times \\frac{4}{9} \\times \\frac{1}{27} = \\frac{40}{243}$$\n\nThe probability of getting exactly **two heads** in 5 flips is:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\dfrac{40}{243}}$$",
